# The parameter space is kept out of the index by `noindex, follow` on the page
# itself (JD-1696), not from here. A `Disallow` cannot remove a URL Google has
# already indexed — it only stops the re-crawl that would let Google SEE the
# noindex, so the URL stays in the index without content. That is what put
# 255,173 URLs in GSC's "Indexed, though blocked by robots.txt" bucket, still
# climbing when it was measured (220k eleven days earlier).
#
# Removing these lines is therefore the point: Googlebot fetches the filtered,
# keyword and paginated variants, reads `noindex` and drops them. The
# deep-pagination pair below (`Disallow: /*?o=` plus the `?o=1..9` Allow list)
# went with them — dominion noindexes EVERY page past the first, so blocking
# the deeper ones only froze them in the index.
#
# Precondition, verified before this shipped: every visitor host must serve the
# listing from dominion, because that is the only place `shouldNoindexListing`
# is wired in. A monolith-served host answers with `noarchive` alone, so
# unblocking it would make its parameter space crawlable AND indexable.
#
